W: 1. The old wife she sent to the miller her daughter,
W: To grind her grist quickly and so returned back.
W: The miller so work'd it that in eight months after
W: Her belly was filled as well as her sack.
W: The miller so pleased her that when she came home
W: She gap'd like a stuck pig and stared like a mome.
W: She hoyden'd, she scamper'd she hollered and whoop'd
W: And all the day long, this, this was her song:
W: "Was ever a maiden so lericompoop'd?"
W:
W: 2. "Oh Nellie," cried Celie, "thy clothes are so mealy,
W: Both backside and belly are rumpled all o'er,
W: You mope now and slabber, why, what a pox ails you?
W: I'll go to the miller and know you the more."
W: She went and the miller did grinding so ply,
W: She came cutting capers a foot and half high,
W: She waddled, she straddled, and hollered and whoop'd,
W: And all the day long, this, this was her song:
W: "Was ever a maiden so lericompoop'd?"
W:
W: 3. Then Mary, mild Mary, the third of the number,
W: Would fain know the cause they so jigg'd it about,
W: The miller her wishes long would not encumber,
W: But in the old manner the secret found out.
W: Thus Celie and Nellie and Mary the mild,
W: Were all about harvest-time heavy with child.
W: They danced in the hay and they hollered and whoop'd,
W: And all the day long, this, this was their song:
W: "Were ever three sisters so lericompoop'd?"
W:
W: 4. And when they were big they did stare at each other,
W: And crying, "Oh, sisters, what shall we now do?
W: For all our young bantlings we have but one father,
W: And they in month time will all come to town too;
W: Oh, why did we run in such haste to the mill?
W: To Robin, who always the toll dish would fill;
W: He bump'd up our bellies!" Then hollered and whoop'd,
W: And all the day long, this, this was their song:
W: "Were ever three sisters so lericompoop'd?"
%
